{"builder":"Goal: add a --tsv flag to the CLI so tab-separated input works, and document it. The pull request must meet both of these acceptance lines:\n1. \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\n2. \"README.md documents --tsv\"\n\nSteps:\n1. Read bin/csv2json.js, src/parse.js, test/csv.test.js and README.md. parse(text, sep = ',') in src/parse.js already takes a separator argument, and bin/csv2json.js currently calls parse on stdin with no arguments.\n2. In bin/csv2json.js, check process.argv for --tsv. If it is present, call parse(input, '\\t'). If it is absent, keep the current comma behaviour exactly as it is. Read stdin the same way in both cases and print the same output format.\n3. In test/csv.test.js, add a test that runs `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` with input 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' and checks that the output equals what the CLI prints for 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' with no flag ('[{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}]\\n'). A table with a few rows and columns is better, if you want one.\n4. In README.md, add a usage line such as `node bin/csv2json.js --tsv < table.tsv` and a sentence saying that --tsv reads tab-separated input.\n5. Run `npm test` and make sure every test passes. Also check by hand: `printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv` and `printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js` should print identical output.\n6. Open one pull request that contains these changes.","verifier":"Check out the PR head and run each check below from the repository root, in order.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"node bin/csv2json.js --tsv reads tab-separated input on stdin and prints the same JSON array that node bin/csv2json.js prints for the same table as CSV\"\nRun:\n  printf 'name\\tage\\tcity\\nann\\t30\\tparis\\nbob\\t25\\toslo\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/v_tsv.out\n  printf 'name,age,city\\nann,30,paris\\nbob,25,oslo\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/v_csv.out\n  printf 'a\\tb\\n1\\t2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js --tsv > /tmp/v_tsv2.out\n  printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js > /tmp/v_csv2.out\nParse each output as JSON (for example with node -e 'console.log(JSON.stringify(JSON.parse(require(\"fs\").readFileSync(process.argv[1],\"utf8\"))))' FILE).\nPASS: each TSV output parses as a JSON array, and it is deep-equal to the CSV output for the same table (/tmp/v_tsv.out vs /tmp/v_csv.out, and /tmp/v_tsv2.out vs /tmp/v_csv2.out). The first pair should be an array of two objects with keys name, age, city.\nFAIL: either --tsv run produces no JSON array, or its array differs from the CSV array for the same table (for example a single key that contains tabs).\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"README.md documents --tsv\"\nRun: grep -n -- '--tsv' README.md, and read the surrounding text.\nPASS: README.md mentions --tsv and says or shows that it is for tab-separated input (a usage line or a sentence is enough).\nFAIL: README.md does not mention --tsv, or the mention does not say what the flag does.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail):\n- Run npm test and report the result. Mention whether the PR adds a test for --tsv.\n- Confirm that the plain CSV output without the flag is unchanged: printf 'a,b\\n1,2\\n' | node bin/csv2json.js should still print [{\"a\":\"1\",\"b\":\"2\"}] followed by a newline.\n- Note whether the byte-for-byte output (not just the parsed JSON) is identical between the TSV and CSV runs."}